# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-autocad-vbscript-comments — the emitted COM bridge opens with Lisp `;;' comments, so cscript fails at line 1
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: cad-runtime
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: schms-ci-and-p3

* Context

Reported by pjb on 2026-08-12, as a row in =issues/open/TRIAGE.org=:

  "ALFE 1.8.3 emits bridge-autocad.vbs with Lisp =;;= comments; cscript
   fails at line 1 before AutoCAD COM initialization. Reproduce with
   =--autocad --cad acad-2022 --mode automation=."

The =.issue= file itself was never created — only the index row. Written up
here on 2026-08-14 while working the Windows-runner issues, and fixed in the
same pass.

* Problem

=*BRIDGE-AUTOCAD-VBS-TEMPLATE*= (=autolisp-front-end/source/backend-autocad.lisp=)
opened with:

: ;; AutoCAD COM bridge — emitted by alfe.backend.autocad
: ;; Placeholders: ${RUNLSPFILE}, ${STATUSFILE}, ${ERRFILE},
: ;; ...

=;;= is Lisp. VBScript has no such comment syntax — a comment is an
apostrophe or =REM=. Because these are the FIRST lines of the file, the
failure does not degrade into something diagnosable further along: cscript
raises a syntax error at line 1, before AutoCAD COM is touched at all. Every
=--mode automation= run against AutoCAD dies there.

* The ticket named AutoCAD; BricsCAD had it too

=*BRIDGE-BRICSCAD-VBS-TEMPLATE*= (=backend-bricscad.lisp=) carried the
identical defect in the identical position — the header is a copy. It had
not been reported because the BricsCAD automation path on Windows had not
been exercised (the macOS route does not use VBScript, and
=verify:bricscad-hidden-ui:windows= drives PowerShell, not the bridge).

Fixing only the reported one would have left a live copy of the same bug
behind a lane that is about to start running.

* A second, latent defect in the same lines

The headers also contained an em dash (=—=). =EMIT-BRIDGE-VBS= writes the
file as UTF-8 *without a BOM*, and cscript reads a =.vbs= as ANSI unless one
is present, so any non-ASCII character arrives mangled. Inside a comment
that is merely ugly — but it is one edit away from being inside code, and
the file already had a second em dash further down, on a legitimately
commented line. Both are now ASCII.

This is the same class of trap as the tag it sits beside: the *comment
marker* and the *encoding* are both properties of how the receiving tool
parses the file, and neither is visible from the Lisp side.

* Fix (clautolisp 1.8.32+)

- Both templates' comment lines use a plain ASCII apostrophe, the VBScript
  comment marker.
- Both headers are pure ASCII, with a comment recording WHY (the missing
  BOM), so the next editor does not reintroduce a typographic dash.

* Verified

Three new tests in =autolisp-front-end/tests/backend-cad-tests.lisp=, run
against the EMITTED FILE rather than the template constant — what cscript
parses is the file, and a substitution could in principle introduce a line
the template never had:

- =autocad-emitted-vbs-is-valid-vbscript= — no line begins with =;=, and the
  whole file is ASCII;
- =bricscad-emitted-vbs-is-valid-vbscript= — the same for the copy;
- =vbs-offending-line-detector-actually-detects= — proves the detector fires
  on both defects it is meant to catch. A check that cannot fail is worth
  nothing.

The alfe SBCL suite reports 589 checks, =Fail: 0=, with the three new test
names present in the log — checked by NAME rather than by exit status,
because =make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl= is known to exit 0 even when a
test fails (=alfe-bricscad-argv-template-ccl.issue=).

The defect itself was confirmed present before the fix by reading the
templates out of the previous commit: both opened with =";; …"=.

* Not verified here

An actual =cscript= run. That needs the Windows runner with AutoCAD, and no
job could reach it while the CAD-capable jobs were tagged =bricscad= /
=autocad= — tags no runner carries. That is fixed in the same branch (one
=cad= tag, concurrency=1, per pjb), so =encoding:experiment:autocad:windows=
and the vendor probes can now be scheduled; this fix is what they need in
place first.
